(a) Any person who abuses an animal, or aids another person in injuring or abusing an animal, or causes or permits an animal to injure or abuse another animal, by acting or failing to act, shall be in violation of this chapter. Cruelty to an animal includes, but is not limited to:
(1) Abandonment;
(2) Failure to maintain adequate care, which is defined as failing to provide for the needs of an animal, including but not limited to, the provision of food; water; shelter; bedding; ventilation; temperature control; space; sanitary, safe and healthy conditions; or adequate and timely veterinary medical attention necessary to maintain good health for the specific age, size, species, and breed of animal;
(3) Leaving an animal unattended in a parked vehicle without proper ventilation to prevent the animal from suffering physical distress: (i) from dangerous temperatures, (ii) from lack of food, or (iii) from lack of water;
(4) Failure to provide sufficient grooming to the extent that said failure endangers the health and wellbeing of the animal;
(5) Willfully placing within reach of any domestic animals any substance that is poisonous or harmful to the animal;
(6) Tethering an animal to a stationary object such that it denies the animal access to shelter, food or water;
(7) Causing, instigating or permitting combat between animals; or
(8) Otherwise treating any animal in a cruel or inhumane manner.
(b) Exceptions:
(1) Licensed veterinarians who perform surgery or euthanasia per the guidelines of the Georgia Veterinary Medical Association and the Georgia Department of Agriculture are not in violation of this section.
(2) A person shall not be guilty of cruelty when the person is protecting his life, the life of another person or animal that is being attacked by an animal that is attempting to severely harm or kill him, provided that the person being attacked is not committing a crime or is not aggravating or inciting the animal to attack, and has not aggravated, injured, abused, or assaulted the animal in the past.
(Code 1990, § 11-3-1; Ord. No. 490, 3-18-1991; Ord. No. 1298, § 1, 10-19-2022)

